Data model
==========

PostgreSQL via Sequelize. Model files are in src/models/; relations are
declared in src/models/associations.ts and applied once at boot.

Naming: model classes are PascalCase, table names are snake_case plural,
primary keys are named after the entity (userId, challengeId, badgeID) rather
than a bare id, except on join and rule tables where id is used.


Identity
--------

users (User.ts)
	userId PK, firstName, lastName, email unique, password (bcrypt hash),
	role ("student" | "admin"), faculty, nationality, totalXp (float,
	default 0), emailVerified (bool).

	faculty stores the full display string from the Faculty enum, not a code.
	The enum has nine values plus Admin. serializers.ts maps each display
	string to a stable theme key faculty1..faculty9; that key drives avatar
	art and accent colour in both clients. If a faculty name is ever edited,
	the map in serializers.ts must be edited in the same commit or every
	affected user silently falls back to faculty9.

	There is no avatar column. Avatar identity is derived: faculty key selects
	the art set, level selects the tier within it.

admins (Admin.ts)
	adminId PK, name, email unique, password, role (defaults to TLC_STAFF).
	Separate table from users. Admin login is a separate endpoint.

user_stats (UserStats.ts)
	statsId PK, userId unique, xp, currentStreak, longestStreak.
	Legacy. The live profile endpoint derives streak and level from session
	history and users.totalXp instead, so these columns are not authoritative.
	See docs/maintenance.md before writing to this table.

refresh_tokens, password_reset_tokens, email_verification_tokens
	userId, a hash column, expiresAt, usedAt/revokedAt. Raw tokens and OTP
	codes are never stored, only hashes.

push_tokens (PushToken.ts)
	ownerId, ownerRole ("student" | "admin"), token (ExponentPushToken[...]),
	platform. One row per device per account.


Activity catalogue
------------------

activity_areas (ActivityArea.ts)
	areaId PK, key unique, name, icon, accent.
	The facility areas the TLC advertises: pool, fitness centre, group
	fitness, open rec and courts, and so on.

activity_sub_activities (ActivitySubActivity.ts)
	subId PK, areaId FK, key, name, icon, hint, promotedFromOther.
	Unique on (areaId, key). The individual things a student can log inside
	an area. promotedFromOther marks entries that started life as a
	free-text custom activity and were later adopted into the catalogue.

activities (Activity.ts)
	activityId PK, activityName, activityImage, activityDescription, units,
	pointsPerUnit, category.
	The older points-per-unit catalogue used by the activity award endpoint
	and the activity badge rules. It is a separate axis from the area /
	sub-activity tree above; the two are not joined.

custom_activities (CustomActivity.ts)
	id PK, userId FK, key, name, pinned, lastUsedAt. Unique on (userId, key).
	Per-student free-text activities plus the pin state that drives the home
	screen stat tiles.


Logging
-------

exercise_sessions (ExerciseSession.ts)
	sessionId PK, userId FK, activityId FK nullable, exerciseKey,
	exerciseName, areaKey, quantity, unit, durationMin, points, performedAt.

	This is the primary activity ledger and the table most read paths depend
	on. Stats, the derived streak, exercise badges and the gym check-in all
	write or read here. A gym check-in is stored as an ExerciseSession with
	exerciseKey "gym-checkin".

activity_logs (ActivityLog.ts)
	id PK, userId FK, activityId FK, date, unitsLogged, pointsPerUnit.
	The parallel ledger for the points-per-unit Activity catalogue.
	pointsPerUnit is snapshotted at log time so later rate changes do not
	retroactively rescore history.

	Two ledgers exist for historical reasons. Anything user-facing reads
	exercise_sessions. Do not add a third.


Challenges
----------

challenges (Challenge.ts)
	challengeId PK, challengeName, challengeImage, challengeDescription,
	startDate, endDate, status (default "active"), venue, instructorName,
	challengeUnit, pointsPerUnit, category, type, goal, xpReward,
	podiumFirst/Second/Third (defaults 500/300/150), requiresValidation,
	createdBy, participantsCount.

	participantsCount is denormalised. It is maintained by the register
	handler; nothing recomputes it, so a direct DELETE against
	challenge_participants will leave it wrong.

	season is not stored. It is computed from startDate by utils/season.ts
	(Winter Dec-Feb, Spring Mar-May, Summer Jun-Aug, Fall Sep-Nov, December
	rolling into the next year) and mirrored in the admin frontend. Both
	copies must agree.

challenge_participants (ChallengeParticipant.ts)
	participantId PK, userId FK, challengeId FK, unique on (userId,
	challengeId), status ("pending" | "approved" | "rejected"), submitted_at,
	reviewed_at, reviewed_by, pointsSubmitted, pointsAwarded.

	This single row is both the join row and the validation record. A student
	joining creates it; submitting a result sets pointsSubmitted and status
	pending; staff approval sets pointsAwarded, reviewed_at, reviewed_by.
	Re-submission after a decision is allowed and resets status to pending.


Quests
------

quests (Quest.ts)
	questId PK, title, xp, category (default "Lifestyle").
	The bank staff maintain. Not a per-day record.

daily_quest_overrides (DailyQuestOverride.ts)
	id PK, dateKey "YYYY-MM-DD" unique, questIds JSONB array.
	Present only for dates staff have hand-picked. Absence means the
	deterministic selection applies. See docs/gamification.md.

	There is no table recording that a student completed a quest. Quest claim
	writes XP straight onto users.totalXp. This is the single largest gap in
	the model; see docs/maintenance.md.


Badges
------

badges (Badges.ts)
	badgeID PK, badgeName, badgeImage nullable, badgeDescription,
	awardXpValue, secret, badgeType.
	The catalogue row. Every badge, whatever its source, has one.

user_badges (UserBadge.ts)
	id PK, userId FK, badgeID FK, unique on (userId, badgeID), timestamps.
	The award ledger. createdAt is when the student earned it. Deleting a
	badge cascades these rows away; XP already granted is not clawed back.

Rule tables, one per badge family, each pointing at a badges row:

	activity_badges     scope, activityId, category, metric, tier, threshold
	exercise_badges     scope, targetKey, metric, tier, threshold
	quest_badges        metric, completionMode, tier, threshold
	specialty_badges    ruleKey (unique per badge)
	challenge_position_badges   challengeId, position, unique per pair

	Splitting the rules out keeps badges generic and lets each family have
	the columns it actually needs. A badge row without a matching rule row is
	never awarded automatically.


Relations summary
-----------------

	User 1:1 UserStats
	User 1:N ExerciseSession, ActivityLog, CustomActivity, ChallengeParticipant
	User N:M Badge through UserBadge
	User 1:N RefreshToken, PasswordResetToken, EmailVerificationToken
	Challenge 1:N ChallengeParticipant
	Challenge 1:N ChallengePositionBadge
	ActivityArea 1:N ActivitySubActivity
	Activity 1:N ActivityLog, ExerciseSession
	Badge 1:1 or 1:N with each rule table


Invariants worth protecting
---------------------------

* users.totalXp is the only XP figure any client displays. Everything that
  awards XP must increment it in the same transaction as the row it writes.
* One ChallengeParticipant per (user, challenge). The unique index enforces
  it; code should upsert, not insert blindly.
* Points already awarded are never recalculated retroactively. pointsPerUnit
  is snapshotted on the log row for that reason.
* Faculty display strings are the join key between the database and the
  faculty theme in both clients. Treat them as an interface, not as copy.